WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.384
Third-party API keys should not be embedded in browser-delivered JavaScript, public frontend configuration, mobile apps, or downloadable assets when the key grants server-side privileges.

2
00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:20.861
If the provider key allows billed usage, data access, account changes, or backend operations, the browser should not receive it.

3
00:00:20.861 --> 00:00:29.007
Backend mediation can keep provider keys on the server and expose only the specific application behavior that clients need.

4
00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:34.040
Instead of giving a browser direct access to a provider credential, the ASP.

5
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:42.980
NET Core backend can authenticate the user or client, authorize the requested action, call the provider, and return a bounded response.

6
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.490
Public or publishable keys still need careful review.

7
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:56.291
Some providers intentionally expose keys that identify a project or environment while relying on restrictions and server-side policy for protection.

8
00:00:56.291 --> 00:01:05.099
Teams should still understand what the key reveals, where it is allowed to run, which quotas apply, and how misuse would be detected.

9
00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:15.099
Sample code, screenshots, support logs, documentation, CI output, crash reports, generated clients, and onboarding guides can expose keys accidentally.

10
00:01:15.099 --> 00:01:23.841
A course, runbook, or support article should use placeholders and safe identifiers, not real secrets copied from production systems.

11
00:01:23.841 --> 00:01:26.556
Per-environment keys reduce blast radius.

12
00:01:26.556 --> 00:01:34.503
Development, staging, test, and production should use separate credentials and separate provider settings when possible.

13
00:01:34.503 --> 00:01:39.801
That separation makes rotation, monitoring, and incident response more targeted.

14
00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:51.589
Third-party provider permissions, billing limits, referrer restrictions, IP allowlists, service restrictions, usage quotas, and alert settings should be reviewed where available.

15
00:01:51.589 --> 00:02:01.059
Provider-side controls do not replace application security, but they help limit consequences when a client or integration behaves unexpectedly.
